<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monastery of Santa Maria de Ripoll</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 880</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ripoll Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Monastery of Santa Maria de Ripoll is a Benedictine abbey in the Catalan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, founded around 880 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a>, who was buried there. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliba">Abbot Oliba</a> in the 11th century its scriptorium and library made it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading centres of learning, where works of Arabic science were copied and translated. Its 12th-century carved portal is a masterpiece of Romanesque sculpture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monastery-of-santa-maria-de-ripoll.jpg" alt="Monastery of Santa Maria de Ripoll" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gennadii Saus i Segura · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VIsta_exterior_del_Monestir_de_Ripoll.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
